# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Two rules of this file's syntax, both verified against `wrangler pages dev`
# rather than assumed, because both fail quietly:
#
#   1. A rule may contain exactly ONE `*`. `/instruments/*/build/*` matches
#      nothing — no error, no header, just silence. A `*` may have a suffix
#      after it (`/assets/*.ttf` works); it may not have a second `*`.
#   2. Cache-Control from two matching rules is CONCATENATED, not overridden.
#      `/*` plus `/assets/*` produced
#      `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000, immutable`
#      — one header, two contradictory max-ages. So Cache-Control lives on
#      non-overlapping prefixes only, and never on `/*`.
#
# Pages already answers everything with `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`,
# which is what HTML wanted: a push to `main` is live on the next request.
# Every Cache-Control below is opting a specific prefix OUT of that default.


# -------------------------------------------------------------------- pages --
#
# The pages DID need no rule, until the Pages origin started timing out. From
# 2026-08-19 the zone analytics show 8-14% of every hour's requests answered
# 504 from the edge, at any load, and for `/` and `/instruments/phyllotaxis/`
# on 08-22: 544 x 200 (cacheStatus "dynamic") against 107 x 504 (cacheStatus
# "miss"). `_routes.json` keeps those paths off the Functions runtime, so that
# is the static origin itself, not our code. One page load in six was the
# Cloudflare error page.
#
# `must-revalidate` is the directive that forbids a cache from serving a
# stale copy, which is exactly the copy we want served when the origin fails.
# So HTML gets a Cache-Control of its own:
#
#   max-age=0                   browsers still revalidate every load, so a
#                               push to `main` is still live next request
#   stale-while-revalidate=600  a stale copy may be served while that
#                               revalidation runs, instead of blocking on it
#   stale-if-error=86400        and may be served for a day if the origin
#                               answers 5xx (Cloudflare: 500/502/503/504)
#
# Not `s-maxage`: Cloudflare reads it as `proxy-revalidate`, which switches
# both stale directives straight back off (cache/concepts/cache-control).
#
# The edge half of this lives outside the repo, as a zone Cache Rule
# (rulesets phase http_request_cache_settings, "Edge-cache HTML/pages 120s"):
# GETs on this host, minus /api/ and the immutable prefixes below, are cached
# at the edge for 120s regardless of origin headers, 5xx is never stored,
# serve-stale is on. That rule is what turns a 504 into a HIT; these headers
# are what stop a cache-control directive from arguing with it. Either alone
# is a weaker promise than both.
#
# The cost is freshness: a deploy may take up to two minutes to reach a
# visitor, and a returning browser may show the previous copy for one load.
# Billy's call: "the site just must work and must load."
#
# Rule 2 applies. These are exact paths (no `*`), so none of them overlaps
# any Cache-Control prefix below, and `/instruments/*` carries only the
# isolation headers, which is a different header and does not concatenate.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-while-revalidate=600, stale-if-error=86400
/bypo
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-while-revalidate=600, stale-if-error=86400
/instruments/phyllotaxis/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, stale-while-revalidate=600, stale-if-error=86400


# ------------------------------------------------------------- static assets --

# Fonts, wordmark, dither tile, icons. Immutable BY CONVENTION: to change one,
# ship it under a new filename. Overwriting a file in place leaves a year of
# stale caches with no way to bust them, so rename instead.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Album art. Not immutable — artwork does get re-cropped and re-exported, and a
# month is short enough to recover from without renaming seven files.
/covers/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800


# ------------------------------------------------------------ web instruments --
#
# Everything under /instruments/ is served cross-origin isolated. That is what
# makes SharedArrayBuffer available, which PHYLLOTAXIS needs to pass visualiser
# frames from the AudioWorklet to the WebGL surface through a shared ring buffer
# (phyllotaxis/DESIGN.md §1). Without it the fallback is transferables every 4-8
# blocks, which is a worse instrument.
#
# This is deliberately NOT `/*`. Under COEP require-corp every cross-origin
# subresource must carry CORP or CORS headers, and /bypo embeds a
# youtube-nocookie.com iframe that sends neither — site-wide isolation would
# blank that embed. Scoping to this subtree keeps isolation where it is needed
# and third-party embeds where they already are.
#
# The corollary is the constraint on anything added here: a page under
# /instruments/ cannot embed third-party media. Same-origin subresources are
# fine and need no CORP header of their own — under require-corp a missing CORP
# is read as `same-origin`, which a same-origin request satisfies. So the
# instrument may load /assets/fonts/*.ttf normally.
/instruments/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

# Built bundles live in /instruments/<name>/build/ and are hashed by the build,
# so they can be cached forever. One line per instrument: rule 1 above means
# `/instruments/*/build/*` would match nothing.
#
# Everything here is `<name>-<hash>.<ext>`, the hash taken of the file's own
# bytes, so a new build is a new URL and the year-long cache can never serve a
# stale one.
#
# **That is a claim about the build, and this rule is only safe while it holds.**
# It did not: `worklet.js` and `tuner.js` shipped here unhashed under a rule
# that assumes otherwise. A returning browser would have kept its year-old
# worklet and paired it with a freshly hashed engine — silently mono, no
# retuning, and nothing anyone could do to bust it. `build-wasm.sh` now hashes
# all three, which is what makes this line true rather than merely intended.
/instruments/phyllotaxis/build/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The ground textures, same discipline as the build: content-hashed filenames
# (see ground/NOTICE.md), so forever-caching is safe and a new encode is a new
# URL. NOTICE.md itself rides along immutable, which is fine — it changes only
# when the roster does, and then the reader is following new filenames anyway.
/instruments/phyllotaxis/ground/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# `.wasm` needs no Content-Type rule here — Pages already serves it as
# `application/wasm` from its own MIME table (verified), which is what
# `WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming` requires. It could not have a rule anyway
# without a second wildcard.


# No Content-Security-Policy. Every page here carries inline <style> and inline
# <script>, and index.html is generated by a build outside this repo, so nonces
# are not available to it. A CSP with 'unsafe-inline' would be decoration. Worth
# revisiting only if the build starts emitting external scripts.
